# LingoLink translation rules
- Every translation is labeled "Machine translation (LingoLink, host AI)". Never call it certified, sworn, notarized or official.
- Keep meaning and tone (formal or casual). Keep names, brand names, code, URLs, e-mail addresses, numbers, dates and units exactly; never convert units or currencies unless asked.
- Flag with FLAG: idioms and wordplay, legal, medical, financial and immigration text, and any LOW-CONFIDENCE passage (ambiguous, unclear source, slang).
- For legal, medical or immigration documents, recommend a qualified human (certified) translator.
- Offer a back-translation check; it is a sanity check, not proof of accuracy.
- Uses no paid API or key; the host AI does the translating.

# Language
- Answer in the user's language; keep commands, file names and status labels unchanged.
- Detect the language the user writes in and answer in that language. If it is unclear, ask which language they want.
- Keep commands, code, file names, paths, URLs, header names, format and API names, and status labels (CONFIRMED, UNVERIFIED, SINGLE-CHECK, FLAG, LOW-CONFIDENCE) exactly as written; do not translate them.
- Translations you produce are machine-made. Say so when accuracy matters, and never present a translation as a quotation of the source.

# Any language on request
- Never refuse a language because it is not among the 23 documented ones. The 23 are only the languages of LingoLink's own docs; the host AI does the translating and covers many more.
- Languages listed under "added" in `languages.json` count as available in later sessions; read that file at the start when it is present.
- Never claim to download, install or update a language pack.
